<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Tale of Igor's Campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Lay of Igor's Campaign, Song of Igor's Campaign</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tale of Igor's Campaign is an anonymous Old East Slavic epic poem, usually dated to the late 12th century. It tells of the failed raid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a> Sviatoslavich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> in 1185 and calls on the Rus' princes to unite. Its only known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was found in the 1790s and burned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> fire of 1812.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-igors-campaign.jpg" alt="The Tale of Igor's Campaign" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slovo_o_polku_igoreve_1800.djvu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem describes the failed raid of 1185 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Igor Svyatoslavich</a>, Prince of Novgorod-Seversk, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Polovtsians</a>, the name Rus' sources give to the Cumans, who lived along the lower Don. Other figures of the Rus' world appear, among them the bard Boyan and the princes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vseslav-of-polotsk">Vseslav of Polotsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-osmomysl">Yaroslav Osmomysl</a> of Halych and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. The author appeals to the warring princes of the Rus' to unite against the constant threat from the Turkic East. Igor's campaign is also recorded in the Kievan Chronicle of about 1200.</p><p>The text shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the old Slavic religion side by side. Igor's wife Yaroslavna calls on the forces of nature from the walls of Putyvl, and Christian motifs stand beside depersonalised pagan gods. Its main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> are patriotism, the power of nature and the homeland. Scholars have compared it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland and the Song of the Nibelungs, but it differs from these in its many vivid descriptions of nature.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, claimed to date from the 15th century, was found in 1795 in the library of the Transfiguration Monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>. Its superior, Joel Bykovsky, sold it to the landowner Aleksei Musin-Pushkin as part of a collection of ten texts. Musin-Pushkin made a transcription for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> in 1795 or 1796 and published the poem in 1800 with the paleographers Alexei Malinovsky and Nikolai Bantysh-Kamensky. The manuscript was reported to have burned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> fire of 1812 with Musin-Pushkin's library. Vladimir Nabokov translated it into English in 1960, and Dmitry Likhachev prepared the standard Soviet edition.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was lost, doubts arose about its authenticity, partly because contemporary fabrications such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Ossian were known. The current scholarly consensus holds it to be a work of the late 12th century, perhaps composed orally and written down in the 13th. Edward L. Keenan has argued that Josef Dobrovský forged it, but Andrey Zaliznyak concluded in 2004 that forgery is virtually impossible, noting that birch bark documents found in Novgorod showed that puzzling words of the poem were common speech in the 12th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The publication caused a stir in Russian literary circles because the poem was the earliest Slavonic writing without any element of Church Slavonic. Alexander Borodin adapted the story as the opera <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-svyatoslavich">Prince Igor</a>, first performed in 1890, which became one of the classics of Russian theatre. The poem also became a subject of politics: in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, attempts to question its authenticity were condemned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Tale of Igor's Campaign” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Saint Sophia in Kiev, with its mosaics and frescoes, and the Golden Gate, and a native literature followed: Hilarion's Sermon on Law and Grace, the Primary Chronicle, the Instruction of Vladimir Monomakh and the <b>Tale of Igor's Campaign</b>, which describes a failed raid against the Cumans in 1185.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Transfiguration Cathedral of the 1030s is among the oldest churches in Ukraine, and the <b>Lay of Igor's Campaign</b> celebrates one of its princes.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chernigov">Principality of Chernigov</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaroslav Osmomysl, who ruled from 1153 to 1187, was praised in the <b>Lay of Igor's Campaign</b> as a mighty ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Principality of Galicia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1185 Prince Igor Sviatoslavich led a raid against the Cumans that ended in his defeat and capture, the subject of the <b>Lay of Igor's Campaign</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-novgorod-seversky">Principality of Novgorod-Seversky</a></li></ul></section>
